  "strategy": "Approach and Rationale\nThe current algorithm combines multiple optimization phases but suffers from getting trapped in local optima due to insufficient exploration. This strategy introduces a novel method called **Periodic-Block Coordinate Descent (PBDC)** that models solutions as periodic structures combined with local modifications. The approach is:\n\n1. Divides the domain into periodic blocks\n2. Adjusts block contributions optimally\n3. Uses coordinate-wise updates to enhance exploration\n4. Incorporates fast scrambling for increased diversity\n\nThe core idea is to represent solutions as superpositions of periodic waveforms, leveraging their symmetry properties to avoid trivial solutions while enabling efficient local search. This approach provides better coverage of the complex solution space and addresses the primary bottleneck: local trapping without sufficient exploration.\n\nThe mathematical formulation involves finding a function \\(h:\\mathbb{R}\\to[0,1]\\) with period 2 satisfying \\(\\int_0^2 h(x)dx=1\\), minimized \\(C_5=\\max_k \\int h(x)(1-h(x+k))dx\\). Periodic solutions are expected to perform optimally due to symmetry.\n\nMathematical specification\nWe represent solutions as periodic functions \\(h(x)=\\sum_{j=1}^M \\alpha_j \\phi_j(x)\\), where basis functions \\(\\phi_j\\) are chosen from periodic waveforms. The optimization problem becomes:\n\nMinimize \\(C_5=\\max_k |\\langle h,1-h\\rangle|\\)\nSubject to \\(\\langle h,1\\rangle=2\\) (integral constraint, where \\(\\langle\\cdot,\\cdot\\rangle\\) is the correlation)\n\nConstraints are maintained through projection onto the feasible set: \\(0\\leq h_i\\leq1\\) and \\(\\sum h_i \\cdot dx=1\\).\n\nImplementation Procedure\n1. Initialization\n   - Validate parent solution (if available) or generate random initial candidate\n   - Project to feasible set using: \n        \\(h_i = \\max(\\min(h_i,1),0)\\)\n        Clip values to ensure periodic structure\n        Adjust scaling: \\(h = h \\cdot (2^n_{points}/\\sum h)\\)\n\n2. PBDC Algorithm\n   a) Block Formation\n      - Divide grid into M blocks of size K\n      - Form basis vectors from periodic components (simplified to triangular/sawtooth waveforms)\n\n   b) Coordinate Descent\n      - For each block position b (from b=1 to B)\n      - Alternate between optimizing local contributions and global structure\n      - Use projected gradients with adaptive time constants\n\n   c) Fast Scrambling\n      - Periodically insert random sign flips in coordinate updates\n      - Compute scrambling probability from normalized energy\n\n3. Update Rules\n   The correlated update rule is:\n      \\(h_i^{new} = h_i + \\nu \\cdot s_i \\cdot (1-\\tau/\\tau_{max})\\)\n   where:\n      s_i = random sign (1 or -1)\n      \\(\\nu = C \\cdot \\mu^{i/N}\\) (smart time constant)\n      \\(\\tau\\) is current time\n      \\(\\tau_{max}\\) is problem-specific hyperparameter\n      C is correlation-dependent scaling factor\n\n4. Feasibility Maintenance\n   After each update, enforce:\n      \\(h = \\text{clip}(h,0,1)\\)\n      \\(h = h \\cdot (n/z)\\) where z = sum(h)*dx\n   where dx=2.0/n_{points}\n\nSearch Choices and Variations\n1. Primary Algorithm Variants\n   a) Waveform Complexity: Adjust number of periodic modes (M=1 to 15)\n   b) Grid Granularity: Use adaptive block sizes (K \u2208 {4,8,16,32})\n   c) Momentum Coefficient (\u03bc): Adaptive schedule based on fitness improvement\n\n2. Important Adaptations:\n   - Energy-based scrambling prevents premature convergence\n   - Dynamic basis recombination increases structural diversity\n   - Progressive period adjustment reduces smoothness bias\n   - Dual-time learning rate maintains balance between stability and exploration\n\nThese choices are justified by empirical evidence of forming non-trivial periodic structures and reducing local optima traps. Each parameter allows escaping flat regions while preserving solution feasibility.\n\nComputational Budget\n- Base resolve: n_points=200\n- Energy computation costs: O(n_points) per check\n- Primary iteration: optimized to 1 per second\n- Safety margin: 30% overhead for feasibility checks\n\nMaximum resolvable dimensions: periodic structure requires O(n_points) but block processing scales as O(n_points/M)\n\n7-stage estimation captures all constraints and fitness computation",
